ich was  lepidus neck of the woods to lay siege  on brutus that way assuming cattlers  would also be victorious labradors would  be left with nowhere to retreat and  regroup pompe's siege was lengthy but  successful and upon capturing brutus he  quickly had him executed even though the  latter had surrendered this wasn't the  first time that pompey had one of his  opponents killed under  let's just say iffy circumstances cabo  had suffered the same fate and his death  earned pompe the anonymous moniker of  adlocentlus carnifex the teenage butcher  meanwhile catalyst also achieved his  goal and defeated lepidus at the battle  of the milvian bridge although his  victory wasn't decisive enough to  completely negate lepidus as a threat  once again pompeii the closer was sent  in to obtain a more permanent result  which he got  sort of he defeated lebanese in battle  but the latter was still able to escape  and flee to sardinia where he fell ill  and died so  well mission accomplished  already pompey had amassed an impressive  military resume many other commanders  would have used all of that stockpiled  glory to parlay it into a safe cushy  position as a senator a governor maybe  even a consul in fact that's exactly  what catalyst was hoping pompe would do  given the last decade or so where rome  saw conflict after conflict right  outside at city gates any military  leader with command of a loyal army was  cause for concern after all who knows  what ideas pompeii might get that's why  catalyst told him to pay his soldiers  disband his army and return to rome  where he could be properly feasted for  his victory as it turns out catalyst was  right to be concerned because pompeii  did not want to disband his army it  appears that glory in battle tasted much  sweeter to him than even the most  sumptuous roman banquet but at the same  time he wasn't so gung-ho that he was  willing to make an enemy of the senate  so he searched for a compromise by  seeking out another conflic